          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid+state+physics"} solid state physics
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+of+matter"} phase of matter
            which is
          %ul
            %li
              %em symmetry protected topological
              (“SPT”,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#GuWen09"} Gu &amp; Wen 09
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#PBTO09"} PBTO 09
          %p or more generally
          %ul
            %li
              %em symmetry enriched topological
              (“SET”,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#CGLW11"} CGLW 11, p. 3
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#CLM12"} CLM 12, p. 2
          %p
            one means a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phase+of+matter"} topological phase of matter
            which is
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariance"} equivariantly
            non-trivial, in that it cannot be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+adiabatic+theorem"} adiabatically
            deformed to a trivial phase
            %em while respecting some
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry"} symmetry
            %em is
            trivial, while in case of SET this constraint is not implied.
          %p
            Since, if one forgets (theoretically) the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariance"} equivariance
            = succeed "-equivariantly" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "-symmetry" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p In other words:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                distinct SPT/SET phases with a given symmetry cannot be
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+adiabatic+theorem"} adiabatically
                deformed into each other, in particular not without going through a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+transition"} phase transition
                %em if
                the whole deformation preserves the symmetry;
            %li
              %p
                but they may possibly be transformed into each other this way if the symmetry is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/broken+symmetry"} broken
                during the deformation.
          %p#TypesOfSymmetries
            There are the following types of symmetry groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            to which the concept of crystalline SPT/SET phases applies (as well as to any of their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semidirect+product+group"} semidirect
            combinations):
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -40px -10px 0px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/TypesOfSymsOfCrystTopInsulators-220515.jpg", :width => "550px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (table from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#SS22"} SS 22
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                may be an “external”
                %strong spatial
                symmetry, namely a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystallographic+group"} crystallographic point group
                of the underlying
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystal"} crystalline
                material, canonically
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+action"} acting
                on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brillouin+torus"} Brillouin torus
                (BT)
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi 𝕋
                          %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}^d
              %p
                for example
                %em inversion
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                is the sign reversal
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/involution"} involution
                on
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mi d
                        %mo ↠
                        %msup
                          %mi 𝕋
                          %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^d \twoheadrightarrow \mathbb{T}^d
              %p
                If a
                = succeed "-phase" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+insulator"} topological insulator
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystallographic+group"} crystallographic point group symmetry
                this way (possibly including
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-reversal+symmetry"} time-reversal symmetry
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+crystalline+insulator"} topological crystalline insulator
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                may be
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time+reversal+symmetry"} time reversal symmetry
                or
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charge"} charge
                reversal, which acts as
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                on the Brillouin torus, but in addition acts on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
                by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+conjugation"} complex conjugation
                and, respectively, sign reversal;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                may be an
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+symmetry"} internal symmetry
                which acts not on the position but on the “internal degrees of freedom” of (electrons in the) substance, which are, typically, located at each atomic site (whence also: “on-site symmetry”).
              %p
                The prime example of an internal symmetry are (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+subgroup+of+SU%282%29"}> finite
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroups"} subgroups
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spin%283%29"} Spin(3)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≃
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq
                = succeed "-group" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SU%282%29"} SU(2)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+representation"} acts
                on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electron
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spins"} spins
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interactions"} interactions
                (such as the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin-orbit+coupling"} spin-orbit coupling
                or the intrinsic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+force"} Lorentz force
                due to an external
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+field"} magnetic field
          %p
          #OnTerminology.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (terminology)
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  Beware that some authors insist on using the specific term “SPT” only for items further down in this list. For instance the claim below that SPT’s are “classified by
                  = succeed "\u201D" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+cohomology"} group cohomology
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %a{:href => "#ClassificationInternalSymmetries"} below
                %p
                  The first two items instead, for the case of free fermion systems at least, are expected to have a classification in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+equivariant+K-theory"} twisted equivariant
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
                  (see at
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+classification+of+topological+phases+of+matter"} K-theory classification of topological phases of matter
                %p
                  For the conceptual relation between these cases see
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a{:href => "#ClassificationInTermsOfKTheory"} further below
              %li
                %p
                  Beware that the first articles on the topic (
                  %a{:href => "#GuWen09"}> Gu &amp; Wen 09
                  ,
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a{:href => "#PBTO09"} PBTO 09
                  = succeed "\u201D." do
                    %em topological order
                  = succeed "\u201D" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+order"} topological order
                  %em trivial
                  (in that the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+state"} ground state
                  is non-degenerate and/or the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berry+connection"} Berry connection
                  is
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phase+of+matter"} topological phase
                  is non-trivial (ie. the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valence+bundle"} valence bundle
                  of a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+insulator"} topological insulator
                  has a non-trivial
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+classification+of+topological+phases+of+matter"} K-class
                %p
                  Due to this problem, the original authors argued that “SPT” could also stand for “symmetric protected
                  = succeed "\u201D" do
                    %em trivial order
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %a{:href => "https://physics.stackexchange.com/a/136096/5603"} X.-G. Wen, Sep 18, 2014
                  = succeed ".)" do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/red+herring+principle"} red herring principle
            %p
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#spatial_symmetry Spatial symmetry
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+insulator"} topological insulator
                = succeed "-phase" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-metal"} semi-metal
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphene"} graphene
                is protected by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-reversal+symmetry"} time-reversal symmetry
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                (and inversion symmetry
                = succeed ")," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi I
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                = succeed "-symmetry" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                        %mi I
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T I
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+insulator"} topological insulator
            %li
              %p
                %em Removing
                the
                = succeed "/" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                = succeed "-protection" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi I
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                %em un-protected
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+insulators"} Chern insulators
                such as (modeled by) the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haldane+model"} Haldane model
          %p
            For more on spatial-SPT
            = succeed "-phases," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+crystalline+insulator"} topological crystalline insulator
            %a{:href => "topological+crystalline+insulator#ReferencesExperimentalRealization"} there
            for concrete examples.
          %h3#internal_symmetry Internal symmetry
          %ul
            %li
              The first example of SPT order is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haldane+phase"} Haldane phase
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+model"} Heisenberg model
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+chain"} spin-1 chain
              (
              %a{:href => "#Haldane83"}> Haldane 83
              ). It is a SPT phase protected by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+symmetry"} internal symmetry
              = succeed "-group" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spin%283%29"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Spin
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 3
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spin(3)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electron
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin"} spin
              (often referred to in this context as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+representation"} projective representation
              of
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SO%283%29"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
