                    %mo ,
                    %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \in \{0,1,2,3\}
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#GuWen14"} Gu &amp; Wen14
          %p
            From X.-G. Wen (
            %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/revision/symmetry+protected+topological+phase/1"}> 2013, in rev 1
            ):
          %blockquote
            %p So the group (super-)cohomology theory may allow us to classify all SPT orders even for interacting systems, which include interacting topological insulator/superconductor.
          %p But conceptual problems have remained with this proposal:
          %p
            From
            %a{:href => "#WangSenthil14"} Wang &amp; Senthil 14, p. 1
          %blockquote
            %p this classification is now known not to be complete
          %p#XiongQuoteOpenProblem
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Xiong18"} Xiong 18
          %blockquote
            %p
              Despite tremendous progress, a complete classification of SPT phases for arbitrary symmetries in arbitrary dimensions remains elusive. A number of classification proposals have been made in the general case: the Borel group cohomology proposal
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %a{:href => "#CGLW11"} CGLW11
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %a{:href => "#GuWen14"} GW14
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#BBCW19"} BBCW 19, p. 3
          %blockquote
            %p Although a remarkable amount of progress has been made on these deeply interrelated topics, a completely general understanding is lacking, and many questions remain. For example, although there are many partial results, the current understanding of fractionalization of quantum numbers, along with the classification and characterization of SETs is incomplete.
            %p Moreover, while there have been many results towards understanding the properties of extrinsic defects in topological phases, there has been no general systematic understanding and, in particular, no concrete method of computing all the rich topological properties of the defects for an arbitrary topological phase. The study of topological phase transitions between different topological phases is also missing a general theory. In this paper, we develop a general systematic framework to understand these problems.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            The proposal of
            %a{:href => "#BBCW19"} BBCW 19
            (may not yet have a physics “proof” either, but) is conceptually transparent: The authors assume, as often done, that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+order"} topological order
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyon"} anyonic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/defects"} defects
            is characterized by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+fusion+category"} unitary fusion category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            = succeed "-SPT-phase" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mi int
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_{int}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
            with this underlying topological order is what we may equivalently recognize as:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+class"} equivalence class
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi Φ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Phi]
                of an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-action"} ∞-action
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mi int
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_{int}
                on
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %li
              %p
                the
                = succeed "-class" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-group"} 2-group
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
                from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mi int
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_{int}
                to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism+2-group"} automorphism 2-group
                of
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              #eq:GIntActionOnUnitaryFusionCategory.maruku-equation
                %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Φ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ∈
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi Hom
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mi int
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi Aut
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\Phi
                      \\;\in\;
                      Hom
                      \\big(
                      G_{int}
                      ,\,
                      Aut(\mathcal{C})
                      \\big)
                      \\,,
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (this is essentially
                  %a{:href => "#BBCW19"} BBCW 19, (1)
                  – where it says “group action”, but later from (81) on (
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1410.4540.pdf#page=14"}> p. 14
                  ) it transpires that the correct
                  = succeed "-action" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-group"} 2-group
              %p namely:
            %li
              %p
                the
                = succeed "-class" do
