                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+2-functor"} weak 2-functor
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Φ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ∈
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi Maps
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mi int
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi Aut
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\Phi
                      \\;\in\;
                      Maps
                      \\big(
                      \\mathbf{B}G_{int}
                      ,\,
                      \\mathbf{B}Aut(\mathcal{C})
                      \\big)
                      \\,,
              %p
                from the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping+groupoid"} delooping groupoid
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mi int
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_{int}
                to that delooping
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-groupoid"} 2-groupoid
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism+2-group"} automorphism 2-group
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %p
            This is plausible (relative to the assumption that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            characterizes the un-proteced topological order) since under an “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+symmetry"}> internal symmetry
            ” one wants to mean a global group action under which all other constructions have
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant"} equivariant
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:GIntActionOnUnitaryFusionCategory"} (1)
            is exactly the data that equips
            = succeed "-species" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyon"} anyon
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+objects"} simple objects
            of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fusion"} fusion
            (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+representation"} braiding
            (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braiding"} braiding
            = succeed "-equivariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mi int
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_{int}
          %p
            %br/
          %p#ButNeitherOfThese
            But neither of these proposals connects recognizably to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+equivariant+K-theory"} twisted equivariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+classification+of+topological+phases"} K-theory classification of topological phases
            (TE-K). While the latter may not apply to all cases of symmetry symmetry protected topological phases, it certainly applies to some of them, and it currently stands out among all other proposals on the classification of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phases+of+matter"} topological phases of matter
            as being the one with the most detailed support by theory and experiment. Therefore it would be reassuring to see how any other classification proposal plausibly connects to the TE-K proposal in appropriate special cases (notably for phases well-approximated by free fermion dynamics).
          %p#ClassificationInTermsOfKTheory
            Conversely, inspection of the mathematical construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+equivariant+K-theory"} twisted equivariant K-theory
            (as made explicit in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Equivariant+principal+infinity-bundles"} SS 21
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/schreiber/files/EquivariantInfinityBundles_220324.pdf#page=201"} (4.1.28)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+symmetries"} internal symmetries
            and how the resulting structure has an equivalent expression in terms of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-group+cohomology"} ∞-group cohomology
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+stack"} mapping stack
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+%28infinity%2C1%29-functor"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                \-adjunction
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/InternalSymmetryInTE-K_220513.jpg", :width => "860"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              (graphics from
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Topological+Quantum+Computation+in+TED-K"} SS 22b
          %p Here the mapping stack adjunction…
          %ul
            %li
